 data for  development be more inclusive how do  projects like the inclusive data charter  plan to address this mmm-hmm I think  this is I think this is a really  interesting question because inclusion  of different groups this is one area  where we're making a lot of progress and  sort of a lot of it is to do with norms  and people's attitudes about what's  acceptable in society but if essentially  there are different reasons that people  are sort of invisible to the system one  is a lack of capacity and a lack of  money to capture information about their  lives in some cases it's a little  convenient to not have information about  people either because you don't want to  spend money to improve their  circumstances or in some cases  sadly different groups have reason to  hide you know so groups that fear  persecution or groups that aren't  treated well within their countries and  not necessarily one  to be captured so this is an area that  there's a sort of technical component  there's a policy component and there's a  sort of social justice and advocacy  component so we're interested in all of  those different areas and we're working  at different levels with a number of  different partners to kind of push the  idea that everybody has a right to be  included in data and everybody should  have a right to benefit from social  progress so what we don't want to see is  countries getting better but people who  are the most disadvantaged being left  behind the sustainable development goals  call for the most disadvantaged in  society to be helped first but of course  if we don't know who those people are  how can we help them so we're we're very  keen to see that happen and the  inclusive data charter underpins that  from an advocacy and from a technical  perspective absolutely so the next  question is what are some of the  challenges that nonprofits face around  advanced cloud computing so I can start  with that great thank you so as I  mentioned before two of the challenges  that we've identified that nonprofits  faced are well one financial and two  technical financial being around the  budget the resources to finance a short  term data analytics project medium term  long term really any data analytics  project because once you get past the  storage point and you start you know  querying and developing custom models  with machine learning it can get a  little costly in the long term for  nonprofit organizations so you know  dressing that financial barrier is very  important especially at the get go  because you know program like data  solutions for a change can actually help  nonprofits create a proof point kind of  like a you know a pitch or demonstrative  proposal that then can rally additional  support then help to you know get that  additional support for you know budget  and and financial resources I'd say on  the other side of things is the  technical expertise so do they have  experience working with data analytics  you know increasingly more nonprofits  are looking to get involved I mean for  all the reasons that you know Jenny  mentioned before around data for  development and opportunities to close  informational gaps so the question is is  there someone actually embedded within a  nonprofit team that is a date  scientist or you know has some knowledge  of sequel and also actually connects to  the financial challenge too because yep  actually having a fully staffed data  scientist nevermind to the data science  team can be also costly so you know  again making resources available for you  know self-training or even you know  mentorship also looking at organizations  across different disciplines as well to  see you know if one nonprofit for  example has the financial resources  they've secured the budget maybe they  can partner with another organization  that does have a data scientist and can  work together on a collaborative project  which is actually another area to that  it says that I've heard from nonprofits  it 